From b7a8ef0ad8e81c2b9ebcd04317d460928473b6a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 05:21:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tui): isolate retained notification signature observers
 [daveey]

Problem: sustained generic cached_ro contention makes each notification signature
borrow a cold private connection and rescan all subscriptions/event history.
The installed PR3992 behavior is correct but amplifies read work under contention.

Why 1: generic dashboard borrows exhaust the shared-reader wait, selecting private
fallbacks (deterministic second-thread hold, real SQL RED: four calls =>4/4 scans).
Why 2: connection-local data_version and event offsets cannot cross into those
private handles; PR3992 correctly refuses their shared-memo publication.
Why 3: signature retention was coupled to the unrelated generic-reader domain.
Root (happened): reader/memo custody did not match the notification workload.
Root (not caught): 743d95b4f9 / PR3992 tested that repeated private reads preserve
the shared memo and fresh rows, not that their repeated SQL work is bounded.
Recurrence: PR3992 fixed invalidation/custody correctness; this removes the
remaining reproduced contention cost, not a regression of its correctness fix.
Historical timeout causation remains undetermined: stack occupancy is not CPU%.

Countermeasures:
- A dedicated query-only retained observer per board serializes signature reads
  with bounded acquisition. Generic cached_ro semantics are unchanged.
- Use existing managed open/recovery, exact-connection memo fencing and generation
  eviction callbacks. Revalidate DB inode plus independent WAL and SHM identities.
- Cap retained observers at64; private fallback remains fresh and unpublished.
  Recovery never closes active borrowers. Reset/cap retirement takes lifecycle
  locks nonblocking and retains custody when closing must be deferred.
- Retain subscription/event coverage, schema repair fallback and notification
  semantics. No poll interval, endpoint budget or detector changes.

Proof (pool worker; synthetic/read-thread, not whole-serve recovery):
- Authentic prechange RED:4 repeated generic-contended calls =>4 full-subscription
  and4 event-from-zero scans. Same regression GREEN after isolation =>0/0.
- Additional edge REDs caught WAL-only invalidation and unbounded guarded close
  during capacity eviction; fixed with independent generation/lifecycle checks.
- 48 notification tests pass. All12 exact-anchor mutation controls killed on a
  no-file-retry run; isolated SHM/deferred-retirement tests close two earlier gaps.
- Consumer sweep:63 files,1096 tests passed before the final two additive tests.
- Full scripts/check:21 checks green; final tree is rechecked before publishing.
- Synthetic8815 TUI subscriptions/980663events,6 generic-contended calls:
  baseline76.2584ms threadCPU,6/6 scans; isolated0.137006ms,0/0 scans.
  With six unrelated title commits:77.743567=>0.974411ms,6/6=>0/0 scans.
  Writer CPU excluded. This proves amplification removal, not live frequency or
  sole historical timeout causation. Benchmark output retained on the card.

Natural deployment only. Incident t_e39e99d8b9d544dd retains serving-process
activation, endpoint attribution and fresh unchanged30min detector acceptance.
